"""Introduction and related work (no experimental numbers here)."""

INTRO = [
("h1", "I. INTRODUCTION"),
("p", "City-scale camera networks, traffic junction monitors and building surveillance systems now produce far more video than any server farm can examine frame by frame. Analytics pipelines therefore read only a small part of each stream, and the decision about which part is read is made long before any recognition model sees a pixel [@edgesurvey, noscope, reducto]. For action recognition this decision has a specific consequence. Many events that matter in practice are short: a hand reaching into a bag, a cyclist swerving, a person stumbling at a platform edge. They occupy a fraction of a second inside minutes of near-static footage. A pipeline that spends its frame budget evenly over time will, by construction, look at the quiet minutes far more often than at the second that carries the label."),
("p", "The difficulty is twofold. In time, uniform or segment-based sampling [@tsn, i3d, slowfast] places one frame per interval of length $1/K$ of the clip, so any event shorter than that interval is either missed or seen through a single frame, which reveals a pose but not the direction or speed of the change. In space, video transformers tokenize every patch of every sampled frame [@vivit, timesformer, mvit], and joint space-time attention grows quadratically with the number of tokens. Static background patches are repeated across frames and consume most of that quadratic budget. Long-form recognition therefore inherits redundancy along both axes at the same time, and the two are coupled: the frames that deserve more temporal resolution are usually also the frames in which only a few regions move."),
("p", "Three families of methods address parts of this problem. Adaptive frame samplers select frames with a learned policy, a cheap preview network, or motion statistics [@adaframe, scsampler, ocsampler]; MGSampler in particular places frames uniformly on the cumulative motion curve [@mgsampler]. Token reduction methods remove or merge redundant tokens inside a transformer, using learned scores, attention, similarity or temporal repetition [@stts, tome, rlt]. A third and more recent family, driven by video large language models, selects keyframes or prunes visual tokens by relevance to a text query and by coverage of the timeline [@aks, bolt, vcast]."),
("p", "Each family solves something real. Motion-guided sampling recovers fast segments that uniform sampling skips, and it needs no training. Learned and training-free token reduction cut transformer cost by a large factor with little loss on trimmed benchmarks. Query-aware keyframe selection showed, convincingly, that the choice of frames can matter more than the capacity of the model that consumes them."),
("p", "Their limitations, however, line up in a way that leaves the long-form action setting uncovered. First, the samplers and the token reducers are designed separately. A sampler chooses $K$ frames and hands every token of those frames to the network, while a token reducer receives a uniform clip and can only discard tokens from frames that were already chosen; neither can move budget from a static frame to an extra frame inside a burst. Second, feature-driven selectors, including curvature-based and query-based ones [@vcast, crest, efs], score frames from deep features, so the encoder must already have processed every candidate frame before the selection is made. That ordering removes most of the savings the selection was supposed to bring. Third, and less noticed, non-uniform sampling changes the geometry of the input. A transformer that receives eight frames packed into a one-second burst and a transformer that receives eight frames spread over eight seconds both see slot indices $1,\\dots,8$; with index-based temporal embeddings [@timesformer, vivit] the two inputs differ only through appearance, so speed, duration and rate of change become ambiguous exactly when adaptive sampling is used."),
("p", "The unresolved gap can be stated precisely. When the discriminative evidence of a long video occupies a short, high-velocity interval, no existing pipeline (i) decides, before deep encoding, how a single fixed token budget is divided across both frames and regions, (ii) guarantees that the remaining timeline still receives a bounded minimum of samples, and (iii) informs the downstream network of the sampling geometry it was given. Each missing capability produces a measurable failure: missed bursts under uniform sampling, collapse onto camera motion or onto a single burst under pure motion-proportional sampling, and confusion between fast and slow versions of the same movement under index encodings."),
("p", "This paper proposes KAIROS, a velocity-proportional allocation scheme for frozen-encoder video recognition. A pixel-level motion probe, run on every raw frame at $56\\times56$ resolution with a per-frame median subtraction that removes global camera motion, produces a velocity field over the timeline and over a patch grid. Frames are then drawn at the quantiles of a mixed density that is proportional to velocity but floored by a uniform component, which yields a closed-form bound on the largest temporal gap and a lower bound on the number of samples inside any burst. Only the selected frames are decoded and encoded. Their patch tokens compete for one global budget through a score that combines probe energy, feature novelty and a learned term, so frames inside a burst keep more tokens than frames outside it, and discarded patches survive as mass-weighted context tokens. Finally, every token carries an encoding of its continuous timestamp, of the local sampling interval and of its feature velocity, which allows the network to undo the temporal warp that adaptive sampling introduces."),
("h2", "A. Research Objectives"),
("p", "The study is organized around four research questions, each with a testable expectation."),
("li", "RQ1. Can a pre-encoder, coverage-floored velocity sampler place several frames inside short events, and does that raise accuracy at a fixed number of encoder calls? Expected: a higher burst hit rate and higher top-1 accuracy than uniform and cumulative-motion sampling when evidence is brief."),
("li", "RQ2. Does a single token budget shared by all frames and regions add to the effect of the sampler? Expected: a positive interaction between sampler and budget in a factorial ablation."),
("li", "RQ3. Does an encoding of sampling geometry remove the speed ambiguity created by non-uniform sampling without harming clips whose labels do not depend on timing? Expected: better accuracy on classes that differ only in duration, and no systematic loss on trimmed benchmarks."),
("li", "RQ4. Under which conditions does velocity-proportional allocation fail to help? Expected: little or no gain on trimmed clips dominated by appearance, and sensitivity to shifts in clip length and frame count."),
("h2", "B. Contributions"),
("p", "The principal contributions are as follows."),
("li", "1) A coverage-floored velocity-quantile sampler that operates on a camera-compensated pixel probe before any deep encoding. Two elementary bounds, on the maximum sampling gap and on the number of samples that fall inside a high-velocity burst, make its behaviour predictable; burst hit rates and sampler-swap experiments test it directly."),
("li", "2) A cross-frame token budget in which frames and patches compete for the same allocation through a motion-novelty-learned score with mass-preserving context tokens and proportional attention. Factorial ablations separate its effect from that of the sampler."),
("li", "3) A sampling-geometry encoding, built from continuous timestamps, log-interval embeddings and feature-velocity tokens, which removes the speed ambiguity created by non-uniform sampling. Zero-initialized gates let the network ignore timing when it carries no information. A controlled benchmark with fast and slow versions of the same movement isolates the effect, and transfer to unseen frame counts exposes its limits."),
("li", "4) A reproducible evaluation that combines a controlled long-video benchmark (TRACE-10) with HMDB51 and Something-Something V2, eleven baselines under matched token budgets, idle-stream dilution, probe-noise and frame-count stress tests, calibration, measured compute and seed-level significance testing, all executable on a free Colab instance. The evaluation identifies where the method helps (temporally sparse evidence) and where it does not (trimmed clips dominated by appearance)."),
("tab_contrib",),
("p", "Table {tab:contrib} links each contribution to the part of the method that implements it and to the evidence that tests it. Section II reviews related work. Section III formulates the problem and presents the method. Section IV describes data and protocol, Section V reports results, Section VI discusses them, Section VII lists limitations and Section VIII concludes."),
]

RELATED = [
("h1", "II. RELATED WORK"),
("h2", "A. Spatiotemporal Backbones and the Cost of Long Inputs"),
("p", "Video recognition moved from two-stream and segment-consensus designs [@tsn] through inflated 3-D convolutions [@i3d] to architectures that factor fast and slow pathways or channel shifts to lower cost [@slowfast, tsm, x3d]. Transformers then replaced convolution in most leading systems: ViViT and TimeSformer tokenize space-time patches and factor attention across axes [@vivit, timesformer], and hierarchical designs pool tokens as depth increases [@mvit, videoswin, uniformer]. Self-supervised pretraining at scale [@videomaev2] and linear-time state-space operators [@videomamba] push further toward long inputs, and surveys of the area identify the token count of long videos as the main obstacle to deployment [@vtsurvey]. Long-form work addresses the same cost by memory or by object-centric summaries [@longform, memvit]. A pragmatic alternative keeps a strong image encoder frozen and trains only a light temporal module on top of its tokens [@evl], which is the regime adopted here. The encoder used in this study is a self-supervised ViT [@vit, dinov2], and the temporal module is a standard attention stack [@attention]. None of these backbones decides which frames to read; they inherit whatever the sampler provides."),
("h2", "B. Adaptive Frame Selection"),
("p", "Learned samplers treat frame choice as a policy. AdaFrame uses a recurrent agent with a global memory [@adaframe], SCSampler ranks clips with a cheap saliency network [@scsampler], audio previews guide visual sampling in [@listen], and resolution or early-exit gates adapt cost per video [@arnet, frameexit]. OCSampler makes a single-step decision over the whole clip [@ocsampler], while AdaFocus and its unified successor select both informative frames and informative crops [@adafocus, uniadafocus]. These methods are trained jointly with a recognizer and require a preview network to run over the candidate frames. Training-free samplers are closer to the present work. MGSampler accumulates frame differences and samples uniformly on the cumulative motion curve [@mgsampler]; later variants add robustness and interpretability to the motion cue [@tmcsampler] or use patch similarity for aerial footage [@pmi]. Pixel or compressed-domain motion cues [@coviar] are far cheaper than optical flow [@raft], and systems research on camera-side filtering and configuration adaptation shows that low-level frame differencing already removes most redundant frames in deployed pipelines [@noscope, reducto, chameleon], a theme that recent surveys of edge video analytics place at the centre of system design [@edgesurvey]. Two properties are missing from this line: a guarantee that a motion-proportional sampler does not abandon the quiet parts of the timeline, and any handling of global camera motion, which inflates frame differences without carrying semantic change."),
("h2", "C. Token Reduction in Image and Video Transformers"),
("p", "Token sparsification began in image transformers, with learned keep decisions [@dynamicvit], halting scores [@avit], attention-guided fusion of inattentive tokens [@evit] and bipartite token merging [@tome]; general reviews place these methods within dynamic computation [@dynsurvey]. Discrete selection is made trainable with relaxed top-$K$ operators or straight-through estimators [@diffpatch, ste]. For video, STTS selects tokens in time and space with a learned scorer [@stts], STA prunes by temporal redundancy and semantic importance [@sta], EVAD drops tokens away from keyframes for detection [@evad], and spatial-temporal merging reduces redundancy across frames [@stmerge]. Training-free variants merge background tokens using attention sharpness [@vidtldr] or remove runs of repeated patches before the network and encode the run length [@rlt]. Hierarchical prune-and-recover schemes reach video pose transformers [@h2ot]. All of these operate on clips that were sampled uniformly, so the token budget per frame is either fixed or redistributed only among frames that were already chosen."),
("h2", "D. Query-Aware Selection for Video Language Models"),
("p", "The newest literature selects frames and tokens for video language models. Adaptive keyframe sampling trades query relevance against timeline coverage [@aks], BOLT applies inverse transform sampling to query-frame similarity [@bolt], and relevance-diversity objectives refine the same trade-off [@adardkey]. In 2026, event-anchored segmentation [@efs], curvature of the relevance curve [@crest], curvature-guided allocation of per-frame token budgets [@vcast] and scene-guided motion-aware pruning [@moprune] all move budget toward transitions. These methods share two properties that separate them from the present setting: they rely on deep features of every candidate frame, and they serve a frozen language model rather than a trained recognizer, so the downstream model is never told how the frames were spaced. Irregular-time encodings developed for sequential data [@mtan, time2vec] address exactly that last issue, but they have not been connected to adaptive video sampling."),
("h2", "E. Benchmarks and Evaluation Practice"),
("p", "HMDB51 contains realistic clips with strong appearance cues [@hmdb], Something-Something V2 was built so that labels depend on motion and object interaction rather than scene context [@ssv2], and the representation-bias analysis behind Diving48 showed that many benchmarks can be solved from static frames [@resound]. That observation motivates the controlled benchmark used in this paper, in which no single frame carries the label. On the statistical side, efficient-recognition papers usually report single-run accuracy. Paired tests on per-sample correctness [@mcnemar], signed-rank and rank-based omnibus tests across runs [@wilcoxon, friedman], post-hoc procedures for comparing many methods [@demsar], family-wise error control [@holm] and calibration measures [@guo] are used here so that differences of a few points can be judged against seed and sample variability."),
("h2", "F. Summary of the Gap"),
("p", "Table {tab:related} places the closest methods side by side. No prior method combines a pre-encoder decision, a coverage guarantee, a single budget shared by frames and patches, and an encoding of sampling geometry. The method of Section III is built to supply these four properties together, and each of them is tested separately in Section V."),
]
